Rejected at Step 0 and documented in [REJECTED.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1412.7373/REJECTED.md).

The paper’s Theorems 5–6 intentionally make primitive elements dense among low-degree polynomials and Section 1.2 presents random sampling as an efficient algorithm. The proof guarantees existence but does not construct an individual witness. Thus:

- G fails if the generator searches until a primitivity test passes.
- Track A fails because the paper supplies the randomized algorithm.
- Track B fails because exact primitivity testing is also the only guaranteed route; there is no shorter structural insight.
- V would pass via exact prime-divisor exponent tests.

The quantitative audit found 107/200 random degree-16 candidates primitive over \(\mathbb F_{2^{128}}\); recovery averaged 0.047689 seconds. For \(n=127\), every eligible candidate is primitive, giving guess probability exactly 1. These reflect the density result in the [full paper](https://arxiv.org/html/1412.7373v1).

No generator or placeholder hardening artifacts were created, as required after a Step-0 failure.
